# C++ standards (Envy)

## Baseline
- Target: **C++20**, keep changes **C++17 compatible** unless the project has been bumped.
- Prefer small, local modernizations; avoid “rewrite” refactors.

## Ownership + lifetime (hard rules)
- Use RAII. Prefer `std::unique_ptr` / `std::shared_ptr` for ownership.
- Raw pointers are **non-owning** unless explicitly documented (who owns, who deletes).
- Avoid manual `new`/`delete` in new code.

## Safety + clarity
- Use `nullptr` (avoid `NULL` in new code).
- Use `override` for overridden virtual methods.
- Prefer `constexpr` over `#define` for constants.
- Prefer `noexcept` over legacy `throw()` when touching signatures.
- Prefer `const&` for inputs; move (`T&&`) only when ownership transfer is intended.

## Optional / error signaling
- Prefer `std::optional<T>` for “value may be missing”.
- Avoid sentinel values (empty string / 0) unless required by existing interfaces.

## Style (practical)
- Use range-for and standard algorithms when they improve readability.
- Use `auto` for iterators/long types; keep explicit types when it aids clarity with MFC types.
- Keep includes grouped: standard → MFC/Win32 → project.

## MFC interoperability notes
- For MFC class members: use `BOOL` for flags (locals can use `bool`).
- Prefer `CString`/Unicode-friendly APIs in UI/MFC-facing code paths.
